WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:07.973
For me they're meant to be
thrown around, worn everywhere.

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:11.100
It's something that you should be wearing.

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:12.030
You should live in it.

00:00:12.030 --> 00:00:14.693
And it should tell that
story of your life.

00:00:16.540 --> 00:00:20.323
It's like this personal
journal of experience.

00:00:27.250 --> 00:00:29.390
I'm Savannah Yarborough and we are

00:00:29.390 --> 00:00:30.883
at the Atelier Savas studio.

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:37.480
One of the first pieces that I designed

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:40.010
when I was working for a bigger company

00:00:40.010 --> 00:00:41.040
was a leather jacket.

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:43.340
It was really like flat and
wasn't interesting at all.

00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:45.160
I took the sample home.

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:48.460
I put it in my bathtub and
I wore it while it dried.

00:00:48.460 --> 00:00:49.770
It molded to me.

00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:51.690
It was different from
putting on anything else

00:00:51.690 --> 00:00:53.510
that I'd ever had.

00:00:53.510 --> 00:00:56.360
I met people in the factories
that were actually making

00:00:56.360 --> 00:00:59.823
these jackets and I learned
a lot from those people.

00:01:00.990 --> 00:01:02.840
So whether it's a $200 leather jacket

00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:07.690
or a $10,000 leather jacket,
it's gonna last forever.

00:01:07.690 --> 00:01:09.640
It's gonna change every time they wear it.

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:12.110
It it rains, you're gonna be okay

00:01:12.110 --> 00:01:13.410
and your leather jacket's gonna look

00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:15.193
five times better when it dries.

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:21.810
The first phase of making
one of our leather jackets is

00:01:21.810 --> 00:01:25.300
meeting with the client, select
the leather that they want,

00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:28.600
any embroidery or specific
details that they might also

00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:32.170
wanna add, and we actually
draw out the jacket for them.

00:01:32.170 --> 00:01:35.573
After that, we take 32
points of measurement.

00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:39.170
With the word bespoke, the person who's

00:01:39.170 --> 00:01:41.469
taking your measurements
is also the person

00:01:41.469 --> 00:01:44.670
that's with your piece
the whole way through.

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:49.100
I'm making the jacket
that you will be wearing.

00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:52.023
Physically, myself, I can only
make four jackets a month.

00:01:56.710 --> 00:01:59.430
The second phase, is to
create the paper pattern

00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:00.913
based on the measurements.

00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:03.970
These two dimensional pieces that will

00:02:03.970 --> 00:02:08.123
then be put together to
create the actual 3D jacket.

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:17.670
The armhole and the shoulder,
that's the most complicated

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:21.160
place to fit and I've developed
a really great pattern

00:02:21.160 --> 00:02:23.570
to be able to adapt it for each person.

00:02:23.570 --> 00:02:25.820
It's a really really beautiful armhole.

00:02:25.820 --> 00:02:27.710
As a designer, that's the first thing you

00:02:27.710 --> 00:02:30.250
look at when you look
at a jacket or a coat.

00:02:30.250 --> 00:02:32.313
Like how beautiful does that sleeve hang.

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:40.750
We make a fabric version
which is basically a mock up

00:02:40.750 --> 00:02:43.540
of what their leather jacket will be.

00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:45.810
There's always some
sort of tweaks that have

00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:48.630
to be done before we actually cut.

00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:50.570
Once the needle goes through the leather,

00:02:50.570 --> 00:02:51.940
you can't take back that hole.

00:02:51.940 --> 00:02:54.540
There's always gonna be a
hole where the needle was.

00:02:57.310 --> 00:03:01.160
When I sell a leather,
when they pick their skin,

00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:03.570
that leather is no
longer gonna be available

00:03:03.570 --> 00:03:05.030
to any other client.

00:03:05.030 --> 00:03:07.550
Oh, I saw my friend Joe and
he was in this really cool

00:03:07.550 --> 00:03:10.720
red leather jacket, I want that jacket.

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:12.060
I want that leather.

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:13.990
I won't sell it to you.

00:03:13.990 --> 00:03:16.190
We will find you another red leather

00:03:16.190 --> 00:03:19.163
and you will have your
own one-of-a-kind piece.

00:03:20.410 --> 00:03:22.421
The jacket that I'm wearing is this really

00:03:22.421 --> 00:03:25.100
buttery soft calfskin.

00:03:25.100 --> 00:03:26.350
There's silk lining in it.

00:03:26.350 --> 00:03:29.380
A western yoke detail here.

00:03:29.380 --> 00:03:30.730
Silver zippers.

00:03:30.730 --> 00:03:31.777
When people feel it, they're like,

00:03:31.777 --> 00:03:33.477
"Oh my gosh, what is that?"

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:37.590
The third step involves cutting.

00:03:37.590 --> 00:03:40.620
We lay out all of the skins.

00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:44.890
We examine each skin
for any nicks or holes,

00:03:44.890 --> 00:03:48.630
any scratches that we may
not want on the jacket.

00:03:48.630 --> 00:03:50.470
Some leathers are more distressed already

00:03:50.470 --> 00:03:53.830
and they show natural
grain from the animal

00:03:53.830 --> 00:03:56.353
and so you want that in the jacket.

00:04:01.020 --> 00:04:04.630
How are all 50 of these
pattern pieces gonna actually

00:04:04.630 --> 00:04:08.650
fit into these hides that
are all different sizes.

00:04:08.650 --> 00:04:10.727
Each one was a different animal.

00:04:22.540 --> 00:04:24.493
The embroidery, it's a lot
of fun and it's something

00:04:24.493 --> 00:04:27.550
that we offer most
specifically on the linings

00:04:27.550 --> 00:04:30.040
of everyone's jackets and
give people an opportunity

00:04:30.040 --> 00:04:33.290
to have this like very
personal interior detail

00:04:33.290 --> 00:04:37.230
and it can be anything; their
nickname or an illustration.

00:04:37.230 --> 00:04:40.180
It's this private detail
that no one knows about

00:04:40.180 --> 00:04:42.330
because it's on the
inside of their jacket.

00:04:55.010 --> 00:04:57.110
The fourth step in the
process of making a jacket,

00:04:57.110 --> 00:05:00.270
you have to do things like
stabilize certain areas

00:05:00.270 --> 00:05:03.120
like the waistbands, the
cuffs, and the collars,

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:04.940
scything the leather if it's too thick;

00:05:04.940 --> 00:05:08.040
basically cuts the thickness
of the leather in half.

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:10.210
It gets rid of the bulk so
that when you have seams

00:05:10.210 --> 00:05:13.110
to fold over, it doesn't
add really any extra

00:05:13.110 --> 00:05:16.660
to that which allows the
seams to actually lay flatter.

00:05:16.660 --> 00:05:18.580
It's really just a matter
of like getting all

00:05:18.580 --> 00:05:20.893
of the pieces ready to be sewn.

00:05:26.170 --> 00:05:29.350
The fifth phase is where
we actually sit down

00:05:29.350 --> 00:05:31.803
and sew the pieces together.

00:05:32.850 --> 00:05:34.660
It's not necessarily,
okay we're gonna sit down

00:05:34.660 --> 00:05:36.903
at the sewing machine and
it's all gonna be done.

00:05:38.120 --> 00:05:40.640
You sew one seam and then
you have to iron it flat

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:42.620
and roll it out and glue the seam so

00:05:42.620 --> 00:05:45.963
that everything stays
really flush and flat.

00:06:09.860 --> 00:06:13.250
We just moved into our
studio downtown Nashville.

00:06:13.250 --> 00:06:14.670
It's in a neighborhood called Pie Town

00:06:14.670 --> 00:06:17.030
which is in a very up and coming area.

00:06:17.030 --> 00:06:18.450
I've been here for three and a half years.

00:06:18.450 --> 00:06:20.320
I've lived in London for five years

00:06:20.320 --> 00:06:21.610
which is where I studied.

00:06:21.610 --> 00:06:22.840
I've lived in San Francisco.

00:06:22.840 --> 00:06:24.170
I grew up in Alabama.

00:06:24.170 --> 00:06:25.590
There's a spirit about Nashville

00:06:25.590 --> 00:06:27.510
that it's just really great.

00:06:27.510 --> 00:06:29.010
Within the few years that I was here,

00:06:29.010 --> 00:06:33.230
I felt this growth happen
and it's still happening.

00:06:33.230 --> 00:06:34.980
There's no second thought in my head

00:06:34.980 --> 00:06:36.730
of anything else I would rather be doing.

00:06:36.730 --> 00:06:39.470
There is a leather jacket for everyone.

00:06:39.470 --> 00:06:42.960
That's why I'm here; is to
basically help create these

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:45.940
pieces that even if it's
someone who has never

00:06:45.940 --> 00:06:48.320
been able to envision
themselves in a leather jacket,

00:06:48.320 --> 00:06:50.373
like we can make that jacket.

00:06:55.760 --> 00:06:59.020
The most important part of identifying one

00:06:59.020 --> 00:07:01.330
of my jackets is the visible logo

00:07:01.330 --> 00:07:04.733
which is stamped in 22
carat gold on each jacket.

00:07:07.420 --> 00:07:11.080
The second is the fit, especially
when viewed from the side.

00:07:11.080 --> 00:07:16.080
I create this very specific
curve on every jacket that I do.

00:07:16.480 --> 00:07:17.560
Slightly higher in the back

00:07:17.560 --> 00:07:20.060
and it drops really
beautifully down to the front.

00:07:24.386 --> 00:07:27.323
And then, we deliver the jacket.

00:07:38.220 --> 00:07:41.390
Leather jackets always
look their best 10 years

00:07:41.390 --> 00:07:42.490
after you've had them.

00:07:43.434 --> 00:07:44.920
The most beautiful thing
to me on a leather jacket

00:07:44.920 --> 00:07:48.920
when I see someone wearing
one, is the creases in the arm

00:07:48.920 --> 00:07:52.370
because the elbow like stretches
out, but you can really

00:07:52.370 --> 00:07:54.360
just see like now that, you
know, I've been wearing this

00:07:54.360 --> 00:07:58.510
one, it's always got this really
beautiful crease going on.

00:07:58.510 --> 00:08:02.020
Every experience that
you have in that jacket,

00:08:02.020 --> 00:08:05.103
something evolves in that material.

00:08:09.810 --> 00:08:12.540
It's like modern day armor
'cause it gives you this

00:08:12.540 --> 00:08:14.580
like confidence that you really don't get

00:08:14.580 --> 00:08:16.230
from any other piece of clothing.

00:08:22.100 --> 00:08:23.650
You put on your leather
jacket and you feel

00:08:23.650 --> 00:08:26.963
like you can walk out the
door and take on the world.

